# Topics and publishing

Topics are an optional WebSocket server extension. A protocol that carries typed publications implements <Symbol path="upwell::axum::MessagingProtocol" /> for its body and default codec, and <Symbol path="upwell::axum::PubSubProtocol" /> for subscriber framing. Applications declare destinations once and inject <Symbol path="upwell::axum::Publisher" /> where events originate.

Start with a mounted protocol and controller from <GuideRef slug="axum/websocket">controllers and endpoints</GuideRef>. Generated subscription clients are transport-specific and documented under <GuideRef slug="clients">clients</GuideRef>.

## Declare static and templated topics

<Example title="Declare STOMP notification topics">

```rust context
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;

#[dto]
struct RoomEvent {
    message: String,
}
```

```rust
#[topics(protocol = Stomp)]
enum Notifications {
    #[topic("/topic/system")]
    System(RoomEvent),

    #[topic("/topic/rooms/{room}")]
    Room {
        room: String,
        #[content]
        event: RoomEvent,
    },
}
```

</Example>

A static topic is a one-payload tuple variant. A templated topic is a struct variant: every `{hole}` has a same-named routing field and exactly one `#[content]` field provides the encoded body. The enum implements <Symbol path="upwell::axum::Topic" /> for the selected protocol.

## Publish from application handlers

<Example title="Publish a system event">

```rust
#[handlers(ws = Stomp)]
impl GreetingSocket {
    #[message("/app/announce", send)]
    fn announce(&self, Inject(publisher): Inject<Publisher<Notifications>>) {
        let _ = publisher.emit(Notifications::System(RoomEvent {
            message: String::from("hello"),
        }));
    }
}
```

</Example>

Use `emit` when a full subscriber buffer may drop an event. Use `publish` when the sender must wait for backpressure, and `publish_to::<N>` to bound fan-out concurrency. Choose this policy at the event source instead of hiding it in a controller-global queue.

When client support is enabled, `#[topics]` generates typed `subscribe_<variant>` methods. Their connection ownership, callback lifetime, and unsubscribe behavior remain client concerns.

